":"print"},{"value":"1538-4357","type":"electronic"}],"published":{"date-parts":[[1982,6]]}},{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2022,6,12]],"date-time":"2022-06-12T10:30:00Z","timestamp":1655029800749},"reference-count":7,"publisher":"Cambridge University Press (CUP)","content-domain":{"domain":[],"crossmark-restriction":false},"short-container-title":["International Astronomical Union Colloquium"],"published-print":{"date-parts":[[1976]]},"abstract":"<jats:title>Summary<\/jats:title><jats:p>HD 101065 is a very peculiar star whose spectrum is dominated entirely by the numerous lines of the rare earths. The only other metals seen in the spectrum are strontium, yttrium, zirconium and barium and traces of calcium.<\/jats:p><jats:p>The effective temperature of the star is 6075\u00b0 \u00b1 200\u00b0K or about 1000\u00b0K lower than that of the coolest Ap stars known to date.<\/jats:p><jats:p>HD 101065 is a suitable testing ground for the theories on the formation of Ap stars. The absence of \u201cnormal\u201d elements in the atmosphere of the star puts stringent limitations on the choice of the available theories.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.1017\/s0252921100080660","type":"journal-article","created":{"date-parts":[[2016,7,21]],"date-time":"2016-07-21T08:52:13Z","timestamp":1469091133000},"page":"351-355","source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":1,"title":["Hd 101065 - An Astronomical Enigma"],"prefix":"10.1017","volume":"32","author":[{"given":"A.","family":"Przybylski","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"member":"56","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2016,4,12]]},"reference":[{"key":"S0252921100080660_ref007","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1038\/211055a0"},{"key":"S0252921100080660_ref006","year":"1976","journal-title":"I.A.U. Symposium"},{"key":"S0252921100080660_ref005","year":"1976","journal-title":"M.N.R.A.S."},{"key":"S0252921100080660_ref001","year":"1971","journal-title":"private communication"},{"key":"S0252